WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.090
It's Wednesday, October 28, and this is the Singularity Daily.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.820
Here's what happened in AI in the last 24 hours.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.550
Today, AI did the math.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.680
Two labs say their models disproved a 27-year-old idea in computer science.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.950
OpenAI went first. Hours later, Anthropic posted its own proof.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.900
It says some logic puzzles get exponentially slower as they grow.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.570
The AI found a faster way.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:44.730
MIT's Ryan Williams is helping check it. He says a few parts look, quote, alien.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.390
OpenAI also claims a geometry puzzle solved in every dimension,

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.050
and part of a $1 million physics problem.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.000
Your passwords? OpenAI says it checked,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.030
and it's reasonably confident nothing breaks.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.760
Now, the money. Nvidia's putting $3 billion into Meta's giant share sale.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.490
AMD, another billion.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.730
Remember Musk's warning that most AI clouds will die?

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.710
General Compute just raised $100 million anyway.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.110
Its boss calls crash fears, quote, massively overstated.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.830
People ask him for chips every week.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.340
And Apple's adding an AI assistant to Pages,

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.140
Keynote and Numbers, built on its own models and OpenAI's.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.650
Meanwhile, Google's Genie 4 builds a photoreal world you can change mid-game,

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:35.610
just by typing.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.730
It even makes the sound: music, background noise,

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:44.480
even dialogue. And a scene holds steady for two minutes.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.070
The catch? Cohere says Alibaba's Qwen model tried to send a file to Beijing.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:56.430
And Musk's AI Odyssey film got one star. Meaningless as a laptop screensaver.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.370
So... is it so over?

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.450
We're so back!

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:06.300
In Shanghai, about 1,000 humanoid robots competed at real tasks,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.590
from factories to everyday life.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.270
Swiss researchers taught a humanoid to climb ladders,

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.550
crawl, and get back up after falls.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.450
And Waymo just nearly doubled its San Diego map,

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.690
out to La Jolla and the university.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.690
If AI does the math now, our job is asking better questions.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.240
Zoom out, and the line only goes up.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.000
Check the board: the future is running early.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.030
That was 24 hours. Tomorrow will be faster.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.200
You've been watching the Singularity Daily.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.310
Stay curious. See you tomorrow.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.480
Want the whole month? Join Peter Xing live in Sydney on October 30.
